title: What a flush reports
tags: [[Watcher]]
kind: reference
summary: Every flush that applied something writes one diagnostics line — events, time, revisions, ineligible paths turned away, and the first few events.

A flush with something to apply records ''one line'' in the diagnostics feed (`info`,
scope `executor`, [[Health and diagnostics]]), which the GUI's message panel shows:

```
flush on photos: 2 events in 3 ms -> nothing written (2 ignored); touched /x, modified /y
```

The repository, the number of events ''after compaction'', the time, the revisions
written, the paths turned away as ineligible, and the first events (the rest counted as
`(+N more)`).

The ''ignored'' count is why the line exists. A flush never runs on a timer — the
executor sleeps until the watcher pings it — so a flush means the watcher really saw
something; one that wrote nothing would otherwise look like a flush for no reason. The
line names the path responsible. Since ineligible events are dropped at ingestion
([[Event batching]]) such flushes are rarer: while that filter is off, or for a path
whose rules changed between ingestion and the flush.

A flush with an empty buffer reports nothing and registers no task. A flush that ran
registers a `flush` task, stoppable from the repos panel or `mf task show --stop`
([[Pausing the watcher]]).
